 9 10 and  11 that is looping through here and I  took some artistic license because some  of Picasso's decisions are lost to time  there they're not encoded in the in the  intermediate States to say what order  did he make these changes in right but  if I were to segment it out and make  these changes line by line then I can  get a simulation of what it might have  been like to sit shoulder by shoulder  with Picasso and see him making these  artistic decisions these choices how to  simplify the bull through the last two  states let me walk walk you through it  line by line so he starts out by saying  this complicated face that he's left  with over here let me get rid of almost  all of that I can just use this sort of  leaf oval type of shape but get rid of  the horns and all the complexity in the  face and then I'll draw in some newer  simpler horns and just a single small  line for the neck now I want to this  this front leg it looks kind of funky  there there's a whole lot of structure  there and I can reduce that front leg to  a single line and you know what with the  other front leg I can do that as well I  don't need two lines I can use a single  line and now I have my two front legs  even simpler and then now this whole  complicated mess in the back where we  have the tail in many lines for for the  hind legs I can remove almost all of  that and this one curved Picasso  keeps this one curved but he repurposes  it he's playing games he's exploring  puzzles with it previously that curve  was part of the hind leg now he must  have this idea that that could instead  be repurposed as the tail if he simply  draw a little connecting line and  further on now this this bowl has  already gotten simple but how could he  make it even more elegant and compressed  for the final state well he starts out  by by choosing to get rid of all the  complexity in the in really in the body  that polygons so remove all those  interior lines and also this this  diagonal line that is the the underside  of the bowl it gets a little bit  complicated there where it joins those  two legs you can redraw that line and  make it simpler and they all connect at  a single point there then he actually  changes his mind about the tail said  that what he did before was a way to  simplify it but he's actually able to  simplify it even further and instead  reuse part of that curve of the the hind  of the animal and simply extend it down  and then that becomes the tail but it  actually connects up to the same point  that it was before so he's playing these  games of what are the different ways  that I can connect the dots sometimes  people use the metaphor of cave  paintings how ancient humanity had this  compulsion to to draw images on the  walls of caves well I also think that  you could make a metaphor to ancient  humanity's practice of looking up at the  stars and seeing all the different  images that you could make in the Stars  and different cultures found different  ways to connect the Stars into  constellations there are so many  possibilities Picasso is exploring those  possibilities here he further then he he  removed he moves and simplifies the  genitals there and completes that curve  there now he is saying you know I  simplified the head a lot but I can make  it even  and simpler I can do that the the head  of an ant like Merlot called it just a  simple single circle almost a dot and  these horns which if you saw the 11th  state and you didn't have this context  you might think why are these horns a  funny shape why does that why does that  one go so much farther up than the other  again it's like the like the  constellation playing these games with  these puzzles of how do you connect the  dots  he's reusing the same dots that he was  before but the one horn goes up to the  same point and the other one goes to the  base of where the horns were before and  finally now that he simplified the head  one final step this this complex polygon  in the front he can he can remove that  and make two lines that all come to to a  single point so this is how we can  simulate Picasso's creative process even  though we take some liberties here we  extrapolate these are almost certainly  wrong in the details we don't know which  order Picasso made his marks to add and  subtract but yet we were able to see  possibilities that we weren't able to  see before  so each medium has strengths and  weaknesses each representation has  strengths and weaknesses for example to  see changes at a glance we could use a  different type of diff where instead of  using all the pixels in the image like  on the left which this is this to me is  a beautiful fascinating image to look at  but it's awfully complex it's hard to  see what's going on instead on the right  we could use a more geometric  representation where we're using shapes  instead of pixels the silhouettes of  those states of the bowl or you could  call them concave holes if you'd like so  this representation while we lose some  details in what's going on in the bowl  and that could be a perceived  disadvantage the advantage is that this  way of abstracting Picasso's abstraction  process we're able to gain some clarity  and how that shape  change so you make these trade-offs I'd  like to to show another geometric  representation and again I will show the  jupiter notebook at the very end and the  representation is what you see here at  the very top of this triangle so I'm  going to come back and explain this but  this this image that's at the top of the  triangle that was generated with code  with Python code to say take the tenth  state and then apply a line  simplification algorithm to that tenth  state called the Douglas puker algorithm  which removes points from the lines to  simplify those lines and by applying  this mathematical algorithm the Douglas  puker lines simplification algorithm  could we try to in some way move from  the tenth state to something that  approximates the eleventh state now this  this is wrong that top that top image is  not what the 11th state looks like it  could be interesting it's but it's it's  it's different so it's not it's not an  accurate representation of that 11th  state but it's interesting to see why  it's wrong and to do that I'm using here  Scott McCloud's triangle of abstraction  from his book understanding comics so he  says you can you can make a triangle  that maps out the possibilities of  visual abstraction we're in the lower  left corner you start with resemblance  like this photo of the girl and as you  progress up along that axis then you  have pure abstraction picture plane  abstraction where you're deconstructing  into geometric forms that lose the  resemblance to the original image and  also even lose the semantic meaning it's  no longer a face  it's just lines and shapes and colors  and polygons then there's another type  of abstraction which is along the bottom  axis that moves from resemblance to  meaning this is iconic abstraction and  like you see the face getting more  cartoony where a single circle and two  dots and a line can represent a face but  that is iconic abstraction it still is  the meaning of the face it's  representing that and the challenge here  if we if we map various renditions of  the Bulls onto Scott McCloud's triangle  of abstraction we see that okay we'll  start with resemblance with this first  state of the bull now this this is still  abstracted it's still as some evocative  lines but it's the most realistic it has  the most resemblance so we'll start  there if we move up towards picture  plane abstraction that's the Douglass  puker algorithm that could be  represented representative a pure  abstraction if we move along the bottom  towards iconic abstraction that that  preserves the meaning but loses the  resemblance then the way to represent  this form of abstraction could be taking  a hand-drawn picture of a bull by an  untrained person not an artist from  Google's quick draw datasets where  Google collected tens of thousands of  drawings of different animals and  different everyday objects a crowdsource  this data set so that they could use it  to train recurrent neural networks to  generate new images but again that type  of extraction is a different time type  and the trouble the difficulty with  trying to capture Picasso style with his  unique flavor of abstraction is that he  is not really doing one or the other  he's splitting the difference he's going  his own way his own path and  incorporating elements of both picture  plane abstraction and iconic abstraction  now I will flip over to the Jupiter  notebook 2 to show you some ways that  that we could that we can do this in  code so I will just pull this over to  the other  the other screen here okay so to  calculate the diffs that I was showing  you earlier I can simply use some code  to read in those images the state 10 and  state 11 and I can show those and the  exciting thing about Jupiter notebook is  that it is a it's a repple so I can I  can see the results the images in line  right there when I execute the code and  I can work I can work iteratively so  it's maybe not quite as easy as the  tracing paper but it still enables this  this rapid feedback loop of  experimentation and now to say to  calculate the DIF I need not just shades  of grey but I need discrete values to  represent where Picasso made those marks  on the limestone for his lithography so  I can calculate the mask and now these  are the masks for four-state 10 and 11  and it's a little bit noisy but that's  okay this is rapid experimentation  it's prototyping and now to calculate  the DIF I can simply use boolean boolean  logic set logic where I can say that the  unchanged portion of the image is  wherever wherever there is a pixel that  is present in both a and B the deleted  portions are the pixels that are present  in a and not B the the tilde character  being an operator bitwise operator to to  invert there so again unchanged a and B  deleted as a and not B and then the  opposite added portions are not a and B  and now if I show the unchanged portion  we see again it's noisy but we see this  curve of the back we see the legs if I  show the deleted portion there you're  seeing mostly those interior lines like  we  walk through Picasso removing and  showing the added portions these are  again with some noise the the new lines  from that eleventh and final state and  now if I want to color code it I can  simply define my colors white red green  and black here then I will make an empty  image here of white which there's not as  expected there's not a lot to look at  here it's just an empty white image now  if I add if I add red to the image I'm  simply saying okay use this as a mask  and whatever portions of the image  should be deleted set those equal to my  red color and now if I show it I've  added that layer as if I was working in  Photoshop but I'm doing it in code and  now I have the red portion here color  coded now I can add the green and now  I'm it's starting to come together I'm  seeing these new lines and finally if I  add the black lines for where there was  no difference where there was not a  change between states then I have this  final image here and if I repeat this  process I can do this for all the  transitions between between states  imitating what I did in the very  beginning with my tracing paper but  using the advantage of the computational  medium to to save me some work  especially on some of those earlier  states where there's just a lot more  there it's more complex right now if I  can bring this this line simplification  notebook over  now if I use just a few lines of code  for the Douglas puker algorithm then I'm  actually able to use another feature of  the Jupiter notebook called interactive  widgets where I've defined this function  called simplify that applies the Douglas  puker algorithm and it takes two  parameters the image that you want to  simplify and then a parameter called  tolerance that says how much do you want  to simplify it by and if I drag this  slider you can see that when I start to  increase the tolerance it's it's keeping  the same fundamental shape but simply  mning it out in a way making it more  angular really and if I continue to  extend that then it gets more and more  boxy and in my opinion kind of kind of  interesting here let's see if I can  extend that so now here's in between and  these are these are really higher values  than would normally be intended for the  line simplification because we've lost  the original shape the original line but  that's kind of the point for our  artistic purpose here and so if I take  it all the way to the maximum value that  I'd set for the maximum possible value  of 150 then it gets to this to this line  here this simplified contour which in  some ways is getting closer moving state  ten to something that gets closer to  resembling state eleven because a lot of  the complexity in the back was removed  we see some simplification of the head  and horn although obviously it is a  different more angular shape and so this  was a way to use math and code and  iterate and explore in the repple to to  simplify that bowl but in a way that was  more pure abstraction and does not fully  or Picasso's process again it's it's not  perfect but even the mistakes are  interesting so I think about Picasso's  famous quote about about computers and  of course he was he was speaking at a  time when computers were big impersonal  machines used to calculate missile  trajectories for war and sadly that  often is still what computers are used  for today but they can also be used for  for more right and they can raise  interesting questions they don't just  give answers they do give questions like  how do I try to capture this unique  flavor of abstraction that splits the  difference between picture plane and  iconic abstraction I'm not sure yet how  to do that but by exploring in the  medium of computation it's enabled me to  to imagine some possibilities and to  have an interesting journey along the  way getting new insights into how  Picasso created his bowls so at the  beginning we said part of the motivating  reason for this talk is the is that  Picasso felt stuck even like we  sometimes feel stuck whether you're  working in art or in software or  mathematics oftentimes he makes it look  so easy  great artists and geniuses they make it  look so easy but even Picasso got stuck  and so if you feel stuck if you feel  creatively blocked you can think about  the process that Picasso used when he  was able to save his variations to  explore where he was going and work  backwards don't feel like you have to  jump all the way to the simplified  version but paradoxically you may have  to start with a more complex version and  then pare away at it in order to get  through your creative block to achieve  the simplified vision that you had in  mind all along thank you  you
 

